    {
      "order": 1,
      "title": "App Overview: Routing Entry Point",
      "description": "The loan tracker is a small Django app. urls.py is the entry point: it maps plain-text endpoints and HTML catalog pages to views.",
      "nodeIds": [
        "file:src/loans/urls.py"
      ],
      "languageLesson": "Django's urlpatterns list maps URL paths to view callables via path()."
    },
    {
      "order": 2,
      "title": "Views Handle Requests",
      "description": "views.py serves loans both as plain text (through the service layer) and as HTML pages rendered from the Loan model.",
      "nodeIds": [
        "file:src/loans/views.py"
      ]
    },
    {
      "order": 3,
      "title": "Service Layer",
      "description": "services.py formats loans from the repository into semicolon-delimited CSV lines for the plain-text endpoints.",
      "nodeIds": [
        "file:src/loans/services.py"
      ]
    },
    {
      "order": 4,
      "title": "Data Access",
      "description": "repository.py wraps ORM queries for listing, finding and atomically creating loans, keeping query logic out of views and services.",
      "nodeIds": [
        "file:src/loans/repository.py"
      ],
      "languageLesson": "The repository pattern isolates persistence details behind a simple interface."
    },
    {
      "order": 5,
      "title": "The Loan Model and Migration",
      "description": "models.py defines Loan (member, ISBN, lent/returned dates, due date 14 days after lending). The initial migration creates the table and seeds sample loans.",
